\Babstract{
A \tcode{[[opt_in]]} attribute for opting int,
and a \tcode{[[requires_opt_in]]} attribute for requiring opting in.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

Kotlin has two commonly used annotations that have no C++ counterpart:
\ul{
  \li{
    \ref("https://kotlinlang.org/docs/opt-in-requirements.html#create-opt-in-requirement-annotations"){\tt{@RequiresOptIn}}
    creates an opt-in requirement for the annotated feature
  }
  \li{
    \ref("https://kotlinlang.org/docs/opt-in-requirements.html#opt-in-to-api"){\tt{@OptIn}}
    opts into use of declarations that require it
  }
}

\Bex{
Library code:
\codeblock("kotlin"){
@RequiresOptIn(message = "This API is experimental.")
@Retention(AnnotationRetention.BINARY)
@Target(AnnotationTarget.CLASS, AnnotationTarget.FUNCTION)
annotation class MyDateTime

@MyDateTime
// A class requiring opt-in
class DateProvider
}

Client code:
\codeblock("kotlin"){
@OptIn(MyDateTime::class)

// Uses DateProvider
fun getDate(): Date {
    val dateProvider: DateProvider
    // ...
}
}
}

The point of this feature is to let the user explicitly opt into features,
such as experimental/unstable/nightly APIs that are part of the library,
but which users should use with caution.

I propose to add a C++ counterpart to this feature, in the form of two attributes:
\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{[[requires_opt_in(\gterm{qualified-id}, \opt{\gterm{plain-string-literal}}, \opt{\gterm{constant-expression}})]]}
    creates \dfn{opt-in requirement}.
    This attribute can be applied to any declaration.
    The \gterm{constant-expression} controls the diagnostic level and/or disables the requirement.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{[[opt_in(\gterm{qualified-id})]]} opts into a requirement.
  }
  \li{
    \tcode{[[export_opt_in(\gterm{qualified-id})]]} opts into a requirement
    and propagates that requirement to users of this declaration.
  }
}

\Bex{
Library code:
\cppblock{
namespace lib{
  struct container {
    unsigned size();
    [[requires_opt_in(lib::experimental_signed_size)]] int ssize();
  };
}
}

User code:
\cppblock{
struct wrapper {
  container c;

  unsigned size() {
    return c.size(); // OK, no opt-in requirement
  }

  // Propagate the opt-in requirement of the underlying library.
  // It's only possible to provide ssize() because the library does so,
  // so we should make users of wrapper::ssize() aware of this too.
  //
  // Alternatively, we could use 'opt_in' intead of 'export_opt_in' and promise
  // future compatibility, like using static_cast<int>(c.size()) at some point.
  [[export_opt_in(lib::experimental_signed_size)]]
  int ssize() {
    return c.ssize(); // OK, use of 'ssize' is in a context that opts in
  }
}

void f(wrapper w) {
  c.size();  // OK
  w.ssize(); // warning: opt-in 'lib::experimental_signed_size' required for 'ssize()'

  [[opt_in(lib::experimental_signed_size)]] w.ssize(); // OK, statement-level opt-in
}

[[opt_in(lib::experimental_signed_size)]]
void g(wrapper w) {
  w.ssize(); // OK, function-level opt-in
}
}
}

\h2{Motivation}

Opt-in control annotations could provide a lot of utility,
both to the C++ standard library and its implementations,
as well as to third-party libraries.

\h3{Eliminating the \tt{std::experimental} namespace}

Currently, opting into TS features (that are not yet in the working draft)
requires use of a \tcode{std::experimental} namespace.
This is actually an opt-in in disguse, but not a great one.
It makes it a breaking change to convert the TS code to standard code eventually,
even if nothing about the API changed.

By comparison,
the same could be accomplishing using a \tcode{[[requires_opt_in(std::experimental)]]}
annotation.

\h3{Providing compiler extensions, including future standard extensions}

For standard library vendors,
the new attribute would make it very easy to extend the standard library with new inventions
and with features that are only available in a more recent standard mode.

\Bex{
\ul{
  \li{
    An \tcode{[[opt_in(gnu::cxx29_extension)]]} could be required to use C++29 features
    in older language modes, like C++26 and below,
    rather than eliminating these declarations entirely through \tcode{#ifdef}.
  }
  \li{
    An \tcode{[[opt_in(gnu::libstdcxx_extension)]]} could be required to use vendor extensions
    in namespace \tcode{std},
    like overloads of \tcode{std::to_string} for \tcode{__int128}.
  }
}
}

Currently, providing such extensions is a risk to users who are trying
to use only the features of the standard they are actually intending to use.

\h3{Quasi-deprecating flawed features}

Some features in the standard may be considered undesirable,
even if we never actually deprecate them because removal is not plausible.

\Bex{
\tcode{std::rand} is known for its low random quality,
and could be annotated like:

\cppblock{
[[requires_opt_in(std::low_quality_random)]] int rand();
[[requires_opt_in(std::low_quality_random)]] void srand(unsigned seed);
}

A user could still use \tcode{rand()},
but would need a \tcode{[[opt_in]]} attribute.
}

\h3{Providing opt-in mechanism for third-party libraries}

Last but not least is the obvious use case:
allowing library authors to mark certain experimental/unstable APIs
as needing an opt-in.
Currently, this would typically be done using the preprocessor,
through various \tt{WANT_*} and \tcode{EXPERIMENTAL_*} macros
that may need to be defined before including a header.

This existing practice is a poor-man's solution because it requires
an ever-increasing amount of conditional compilation.
Preprocessing solutions also don't play nicely with modules;
one can't customize modules by putting \tcode{#define WANT_STUFF} before \tcode{import m;}.

These solutions are also not capable of propagating opt-in requirements.
Once some feature has been included via \tt{WANT}, it's simply there,
and the downstream consumers are not aware that they are indirectly using an unstable API.
By comparison, \tcode{[[export_opt_in]]} can be used to propagate opt-in requirements,
and such requirements can be propagated through the type system:

\Bex{
Core library:
\cppblock{
namespace mathlib {
  using float8_t = /* ... */;
  // ...
  [[requires_opt_in(
    mathlib::decimal_float,
    "Support for decimal floating-point is experimental."
  )]]
  using decimal64_t = /* ... */;
}
}

Linear algebra library:
\cppblock{
namespace linalg {
  template<class T> struct vec3 {
    T x, y, z;
    // ...
  };

  [[export_opt_in(mathlib::decimal_float)]]
  using vec3d64 = vec3<mathlib::decimal64_t>;
}
}
Note that without the attribute on the declaration of \tcode{vec3d64},
a warning would be emitted.

Downstream user code:
\cppblock{
void f(linalg::vec3d64 v); // warning: opt-in 'mathlib::decimal_float' required for 'vec3d64':
                   // Support for decimal floating-point is experimental.

[[opt_in(mathlib::decimal_float)]]
void g(linalg::vec3d64 v); // OK
}
}

\Bex{
A a more concrete example,
the \ref("https://bemanproject.org/"){Beman project} provides
\ref("https://github.com/bemanproject"){reference implementations for various standard library proposals}.

For those libraries,
\tcode{[[requires_opt_in]]} could distinguish between features that are actually
in the proposed wording and part of the current design,
and features where the paper author is merely \q{floating an idea}
and experimenting with design alternatives.
}

\Bex{
OpenGL essentially has two categories of functions:
\ul{
  \li{\q{Immediate-mode rendering} functions like \tcode{glBegin} and \tcode{glEnd}.}
  \li{Modern functions like \tcode{glBindVertexArray}.}
}

While \tcode{glBegin} and \tcode{glEnd} are deprecated legacy OpenGL functionality,
it makes little sense to make them \tcode{[[deprecated]]} in code.
These functions are not going away anytime soon,
and they do provide some toy graphics API for educational purposes which is very easy to use.
Silencing \tcode{[[deprecated]]} would also require disabling e.g. \tcode{-Wdeprecated} entirely,
which would be very bad.

However, it would be realistic for an OpenGL header or for some C++ wrapper to provide
\tcode{[[requires_opt_in(legacy_gl_api)]]}
so that old functions are not used accidentally.
}

\h2{Design}

\h3{Controlling diagnostic level}

The third parameter in a \tcode{[[requires_opt_in(/* ... */)]]} attribute is an optional
\gterm{constant-expression} of type \tcode{std::opt_in_requirement}:

\cppblock{
enum class opt_in_requirement {
  // Requirement is disabled.
  none,
  // Violation of the opt-in requirement results in a warning (default).
  warning,
  // Violation of the opt-in requirement results in an error.
  error,
};
}

This level gives library authors more control,
and is analogous to Kotlin's \code("kt"){RequiresOptIn.Level},
which is either \code("kt"){ERROR} or \code("kt"){WARNING}.
The level also allows marking particular specializations as requiring an opt-in.

\Bex{
For a template \tcode{result<T, E>},
there are two ways to make the specialization \tcode{result<void, E>}
require an opt-in:

\cppblock{
// 1. Apply the requirement to the primrary template,
//    assuming that only that template is provided.
template<class T, class E>
struct [[requires_opt_in(/* ... */, std::is_void_v<T> ?
         std::opt_in_requirement::warning : std::opt_in_requirement::none)]]
result {
    /* ... */
};

// 2. Alternatively, apply the requirement to some partial specialization.
template<class E>
  struct [[requires_opt_in(/* ... */)]] result<void, E> { /* ... */ };
}
}

Without the extra requirement parameter,
users would struggle with marking the use of specific template parameters
as requiring an opt-in,
such as when only \tcode{const} or \tcode{volatile} parameters
require opt-in.
This would be especially problematic for function templates,
which don't support partial specializations.

\h3{When opt-in is required}

Opt-in is required in the same cases where \tcode{[[deprecated]]} would emit a warning.
For example, a deprecated overload in an overload set does not mean that the entire
overload set requires opt-in.

When it has been determined that opt-in is required for some construct that refers to
another construct,
the \tcode{opt_in_requirement} value of the requirement is checked,
and either nothing, a warning, or error is emitted.

\h3{Overload resolution and SFINAE}

Even when the user specifies \tcode{opt_in_requirement::error},
this does not alter the result of overload resolution, and it is SFINAE-unfriendly.
Any opt-in requirement is either satisfied,
or the diagnostic is emitted.

\h3{Ignorability of attributes}

A notable problem is that older compilers do not support
\tcode{[[requires_opt_in]]},
so even if a library author specifies \tcode{opt_in_requirement::error},
there is no strict guarantee of an error on lack of opt-in.

However, this problem is not a deal-breaker because
\ul{
  \li{
    the library author cannot distinguish between a user whose \tcode{[[requires_opt_in]]}
    attribute is \q{broken} and between a user who opted in,
    so the functionality they are providing still needs to be correct for everyone, and
  }
  \li{
    a library author could also guard opt-in features behind the \tcode{__cpp_opt_in}
    feature-test macro,
    so they only provide opt-in extras if users are reliably forced to opt in.
  }
}

The feature-test macro is actually necessary to avoid warnings for unrecognized attributes,
so all a library author has to do is:
\cppblock{
#ifdef __cpp_opt_in
[[requires_opt_in(/* ... */)]]
\ins{void f();}
#endif
\del{void f();}
}
That is, they should make the whole declaration guarded by the feature-test,
not just the attribute.
Or alternatively,
\cppblock{
// defined once
#ifdef __cpp_opt_in
#define MY_LIB_OPT_IN_FEATURE(id, msg, ...) \\
  [[requires_opt_in(id, msg)]] __VA_ARGS__
#endif

// Expands to nothing of opting in is not possible,
// or expands to a declaration with [[requires_opt_in]]
MY_LIB_OPT_IN_FEATURE(stuff, "Experimental.",
void f() {
  // ...
}
)
}

Of course, eventually support for \tcode{[[requires_opt_in]]} would be so widespread
that these macro uses would no longer be necessary.
Pure C++29 code would have no need for them.
This may just take a few decades.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5046").

\h3(show_number=false){[version.syn]}

Add a feature-test macro to \eelis{version.syn} as follows:

\Bins{
\itemdecl{
#define __cpp_my_new_feature 20XXXXL // \serif{freestanding, also in \tt{<header>}}
}
}

A \tcode{std::vector} is a \exposid{std-vector}.

